  "title": "Erdős Problem #143",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "Let $A\\subset (1,\\infty)$ be a countably infinite set such that for all $x\neq y\\in A$ and integers $k\\geq 1$ we have $  \\lvert kx -y\\rvert \\geq 1. $ Does this imply that $A$ is sparse? In particular, does this imply that $ \\sum_{x\\in A}\\frac{1}{x\\log x}<\\infty $ or $ \\sum_{\\substack{x <n\\\\ x\\in A}}\\frac{1}{x}=o(\\log n)? $ ",
